  "error": null,
  "output_text": "Below is a summary of the over-representation analysis (ORA) you requested on the 17 ORF1p-associated proteins (15 up, 2 down) against GO Biological Process (BP), Cellular Component (CC) and Molecular Function (MF) libraries. No CC or MF terms passed the p<0.20 filter; significant enrichments are in GO-BP only.\n\n1. ORA summary for GO_Biological_Process_2023  \n   – Total enriched terms (adj P < 0.20): 83  \n   – Strongest hits (adj P < 0.05, sorted by Combined Score):  \n     • Ribosome Biogenesis (GO:0042254): TBL3; ESF1; BYSL (Overlap 3/155; adj P = 0.027)  \n     • Ribosomal Small Subunit Biogenesis (GO:0042274): TBL3; DDX52 (2/84; adj P = 0.040)  \n     • Maintenance Of DNA Repeat Elements (GO:0043570): MSH6 (1/5; adj P = 0.040)  \n     • Regulation Of Post-Transcriptional Gene Silencing By RNA (GO:1900368): XPO5 (1/5; adj P = 0.040)  \n     • Pyrimidine Ribonucleotide Biosynthetic Process (GO:0009220): CAD (1/6; adj P = 0.040)  \n     • Mitochondrial Transcription (GO:0006390): TFB2M (1/12; adj P = 0.040)  \n     • Regulation Of Mitochondrial Gene Expression (GO:0062125): LRPPRC (1/8; adj P = 0.040)  \n\n2. Grouped pathways and key proteins  \n   • Ribosome biogenesis & rRNA processing – TBL3, ESF1, BYSL, DDX52, PDCD11  \n     – Supports high translational demand in rapidly proliferating/cancerous cells and could facilitate assembly of viral RNP complexes in “viral mimicry.”  \n   • DNA replication/repair & helicase regulation – MSH6, RFC4, TFB2M  \n     – Reflects replication stress and mismatch repair activity; may influence detection of L1-derived DNA intermediates by cGAS-STING.  \n   • Mitochondrial gene expression/transcription – LRPPRC, SLIRP, TFB2M  \n     – Modulates mtRNA handling; dysregulated mt-transcripts or mtDNA leak can serve as innate immune triggers.  \n   • Nucleotide metabolism – CAD  \n     – Up-regulation of pyrimidine synthesis supports both DNA repair and high RNA output; may also fuel viral RNA intermediate formation.  \n   • Post-transcriptional gene silencing & RNA export – XPO5  \n     – Exports pre-miRNAs and influences RISC-mediated silencing; could dampen dsRNA–sensing pathways (e.g., RIG-I/MDA5).  \n   • Phosphatase-mediated repression – PPM1G  \n     – Dephosphorylates serine/threonine substrates including transcription factors; potential negative regulator of IRF3/NF-κB signaling.  \n   • NF-κB repression – NKRF  \n     – Directly binds the NF-κB p50 subunit to block inflammatory and IFN-1 gene transcription.  \n\n3. Down-regulated proteins and implications  \n   • SELENBP1 – Normally has antioxidant and anti-inflammatory roles; its loss may exacerbate oxidative stress and NF-κB activation.  \n   • VPS13C – Involved in vesicle trafficking; down-regulation could impair autophagy-mediated clearance of viral-like particles.  \n\n4. Relevance to ORF1p-high tumors, viral mimicry & IFN-1 signaling  \n   – Enhanced ribosome biogenesis and RNA processing (TBL3/ESF1/BYSL/DDX52) mirror a viral-like translational burst, potentially increasing cytoplasmic dsRNA levels.  \n   – Up-regulation of DNA repair and helicase regulators (MSH6, RFC4) suggests active resolution of ORF1p-linked DNA damage—this may limit chronic cGAS-STING engagement.  \n   – Mitochondrial factors (LRPPRC, TFB2M) govern mtRNA/mtDNA handling; their elevation could influence mt-DAMP release and IFN-1 induction.  \n   – XPO5 and PPM1G provide “masks” on dsRNA and phospho-dependent signaling, respectively, dampening innate sensors.  \n   – NKRF specifically represses NF-κB targets, including IFN-β, providing a direct brake on inflammation.  \n\n5. Proteins likely contributing to IFN-1 repression/inflammation dampening  \n   • NKRF (NF-κB repressor)  \n   • PPM1G (phosphatase targeting IRF3/NF-κB)  \n   • XPO5 (miRNA/siRNA pathway, limits dsRNA sensing)  \n   • LRPPRC/SLIRP (mitochondrial RNA chaperones, may limit mt-RNA leak)  \n\nTaken together, ORF1p-high tumors up-regulate a suite of proteins that both support high nucleic-acid metabolism (DNA repair, replication, RNA processing, ribosome assembly) and actively repress innate immune sensors (via NF-κB/IRF3 dephosphorylation, miRNA-mediated silencing, mitochondrial RNA control). Down-regulation of SELENBP1 and VPS13C further tips the balance toward reduced antioxidant/inflammatory clearance. This coordinated shift may underlie how ORF1p “masks” L1-derived nucleic acids to blunt IFN-1 responses.",
